    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "What investment value does $3.6$ correspond to on the log scale?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\$10^{3.6}\\approx\\$3981$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{10}A&=3.6\\\\A&=10^{3.6}\\\\A&\\approx3981.071705\\end{align*}$$The investment value is approximately $\\$3981$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 10
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Which graph can be used to find the investment value at age 20 years and what is this value?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The logarithm graph; approximately $\\$10\\,000$.",
          "worked": "The logarithm graph gives the clearer reading at age 20: $\\log_{10}A\\approx4$. Since $10^4=10\\,000$, the investment value is approximately $\\$10\\,000$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 12
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "By what factor has the investment value increased from age 20 to age 30?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Approximately $2.5$ times.",
          "worked": "Looking at the logarithm graph, the readings at ages 20 and 30 are approximately $4.0$ and $4.4$, a difference of about $0.4$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Growth factor}&\\approx\\frac{10^{4.4}}{10^{4.0}}\\\\\\text{Growth factor}&\\approx10^{0.4}\\\\\\text{Growth factor}&\\approx2.5\\end{align*}$$The investment is approximately $2.5$ times its age-20 value.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "By what factor has the investment value increased when the log scale increases by 1 unit?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "A factor of $10$.",
          "worked": "For logarithm readings $x$ and $x+1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Growth factor}&=\\frac{10^{x+1}}{10^x}\\\\\\text{Growth factor}&=10\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 9
      },
      {
        "id": "p35-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "If a graph using a log scale is linear, what form is the graph of the original data?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Exponential.",
          "worked": "Here the graph shows the logarithm of investment value against ordinary time. Equal time intervals give equal increases in the logarithm, so the investment is multiplied by a constant factor over each interval. The original relationship is exponential.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 9
      }
    ]
  },
